# Trivy misconfiguration IDs suppressed repo-wide, with the reason each is here.
#
# Scoped deliberately: this file is NOT a place to silence findings that are
# merely inconvenient. Each entry has to be a rule that is wrong for this estate
# rather than one this estate is failing.

# KSV-0040 — "a resource quota policy with hard memory and CPU limits should be
# configured per namespace".
#
# Suppressed because it is inapplicable here, and because leaving it on makes
# the required `chargate` check block every pull request that adds a Kubernetes
# manifest anywhere.
#
# Inapplicable: this cluster does not use per-namespace ResourceQuotas. The
# Kyverno ClusterPolicy `default-limitrange`
# (kubernetes/apps/kyverno-policies/base/clusterpolicy-default-limitrange.yaml)
# generates a LimitRange in every namespace instead, with
# `defaultRequest: {cpu: 10m, memory: 64Mi}` and deliberately no default limit —
# "it exists to make unset impossible, not to size anything". A quota that
# declared `limits.cpu` would go further and FORCE a CPU limit on every pod,
# which is the opposite of the documented convention in cleanup.md: "Set CPU
# requests, omit CPU limits — CPU is compressible".
#
# Blocking: the rule is evaluated across the whole scan rather than per
# resource, so it fires on every file in a tree that contains a Namespace —
# 568 times in kubernetes/ as of 2026-08-14, every one of them accepted as
# pre-existing. Any newly added file therefore arrives as a net-new finding no
# matter what it contains, and no per-file fix helps: inline
# `# trivy:ignore:` comments do not apply to it, and adding a compliant
# ResourceQuota makes it fire on every OTHER file in the same tree (verified by
# bisecting with `trivy config`: with a quota, 8 findings; without, zero).
# Trivy check IDs accepted repo-wide.
#
# KSV-0040 — "resource quota usage": every namespace should carry a ResourceQuota
# with hard CPU and memory limits. Not this repo's pattern; the identical risk is
# already accepted at KICS level as rule 48a5beba in each runner manifest, and
# Trivy raises it on 561 objects here, all but a handful pre-existing.
#
# WHY REPO-WIDE AND NOT PER-FILE. Chargate gates on NET-NEW findings, and every
# result in a brand-new file is net-new by definition (docs/net-new.md, "Brand-new
# file → all results net-new"). Trivy raises KSV-0040 on essentially every
# Kubernetes manifest, so a per-file suppression only ever fixes the PR in front of
# you: the next PR that adds a manifest fails the gate again, on a rule the repo
# has already decided not to follow.
#
# THE ID FORMAT IS LOAD-BEARING. This scanner reports the check as `KSV-0040`,
# hyphenated — that is the literal SARIF `ruleId` in the chargate artifact, and it
# matches the AVD slug (https://avd.aquasec.com/misconfig/ksv-0040). The
# unhyphenated `KSV0040` tried previously — both here and as inline
# `# trivy:ignore:` comments — matched nothing, and the failure is silent: an
# unmatched entry is not an error, the finding simply keeps gating. All three
# spellings are listed because the form has moved between Trivy versions and an
# entry that matches nothing costs nothing.
#
# Note that in-source `# trivy:ignore:` comments would not help chargate anyway.
# Chargate counts a finding as suppressed only when the SARIF result carries a
# `suppressions` array (SARIF 2.1.0 §3.27.23) — semgrep's `# nosemgrep` is the only
# mechanism in this repo that produces one. Trivy does not emit suppressions; it
# drops ignored findings from its output entirely, which is what this file does.
KSV-0040
KSV0040
AVD-KSV-0040
